    elif nums == []:
        return None

When the user calls `Complement(FiniteSet(x, y, 2), Interval(-10, 10))` (invoked as `FiniteSet(x,y,2) - Interval(-10,10)`, i.e. `Complement(a, b)` with `A = FiniteSet(x,y,2)`, `B = Interval(-10,10)`), `Complement.reduce` invokes `B._complement(A)`. Inside `Interval._complement`, `other` is the `FiniteSet(x, y, 2)`, so `nums = [m for m in other.args if m.is_number] = [2]` (non-empty), and `other != S.Reals`, so the first `if` is skipped and execution falls through to `return Set._complement(self, other)`. In `Set._complement`, the branch `elif isinstance(other, FiniteSet):` then executes:

    return FiniteSet(*[el for el in other if self.contains(el) != True])

This treats `self` (the Interval) as the universe and `other` (the FiniteSet) as the set being complemented — but `FiniteSet.contains` only checks membership of the `Interval`'s endpoints, not of the symbols `x, y`. The numeric element `2` is contained in `Interval(-10,10)` (so `self.contains(2) is True`) but the symbols `x, y` evaluate to an un-resolvable boolean, not `True`. The list comprehension keeps `x` and `y` (because `self.contains(x) != True`) and drops `2`, returning `FiniteSet(x, y)` — which is precisely the wrong result. The expected `{x, y} \ [-10, 10]` is produced by the *other* path: `Complement` should be computed as `A - B` (i.e. drop from `A` those elements of `B`), not `B - A`.

