output.root_cause:
The defective behaviour is that `Field.__hash__` returns a different value once the field is attached to a model class, because it incorporates the (previously absent) `model` attribute into its hash. The exact offending line is in `Field.__hash__` in `django/db/models/fields/__init__.py` (line 521):

    def __hash__(self):
        return hash((
            self.creation_counter,
            self.model._meta.app_label if hasattr(self, 'model') else None,
            self.model._meta.model_name if hasattr(self, 'model') else None,
        ))

Prior to being assigned to a model (e.g. `class Book(models.Model): title = f`), `f` has no `model` attribute, so `hasattr(self, 'model')` is False and the hash is based only on `creation_counter`. After assignment, `contribute_to_class` sets `self.model = cls`, and subsequent `__hash__` calls include `(app_label, model_name)`, producing a different hash. This violates the Python hash invariant (an object's hash must be immutable for the lifetime of that object while it is stored in a hash-based collection), causing the field to become unreachable from the dict it was inserted into (`{f: 1}` -> `f in d` returns False).

